.superpowers/
.vikunja-mcp.env

# Claude Code local state — keep shared config (settings.json) trackable.
# Use `.claude/*`, NOT `.claude/`: git won't descend into a blanket-ignored
# dir, so a later `!.claude/settings.json` could never re-include it. This
# hides machine-local runtime state (scheduled_tasks.lock, worktrees/,
# mailbox/, settings.local.json) while keeping shared project config.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json

# Stray saved web pages (e.g. docroot.html); no .html is tracked or ever has been.
*.html

# Playwright artifacts (#607). The MCP browser writes auto-named output to `.playwright-mcp/`
# relative to the SERVER's cwd — the main checkout, not the per-task worktree the agent runs
# in. Measured (#585): one navigate produced `page-<timestamp>.yml`, and a page snapshot
# carries the page's TEXT, so a logged-in page ends up quoted in it. With the storage
# capability on, the same directory is also where a storage-state export lands by default.
.playwright-mcp/

# Browser artifacts written under a CALLER-CHOSEN name (#629) — the hole beside the rule above,
# which only covers the AUTO-named ones. A `filename` argument is resolved against the SERVER's
# cwd, i.e. the main checkout, so it lands in the repo ROOT rather than the output dir. Measured
# (@playwright/mcp 0.0.78, own `--isolated --headless` server, own stdio client, throwaway
# origin): `browser_take_screenshot(filename="x.png")` answered `- [Screenshot of viewport]
# (./x.png)` and wrote it there. Against THIS repo's own pre-#629 `.gitignore`,
# `git check-ignore --no-index -v` then exited 1 — not ignored — for `shot-629.png` and for the
# `.jpeg` and `.pdf` artifacts of the same run alike. SKILL.md PRESCRIBES the call that puts the
# file there ("приложи скриншот", `browser_take_screenshot(filename="vmcp-554-probe.png")`, "в
# attach_file отдавай АБСОЛЮТНЫЙ путь В ГЛАВНОМ ЧЕКАУТЕ") — and then, separately, tells the agent
# "Сам артефакт не коммить". So the artifact arrives by the book, and until these rules the ONLY
# thing between it and a PUBLIC repo was that one sentence of discipline. That is the same trade
# #607 refused for the auto-named ones, and the same risk: a screenshot of a logged-in page.
#
# WHAT THESE FOUR LINES DO AND DO NOT DO. They are a list of EXTENSIONS, and the extension does
# not decide the content — measured in BOTH directions, `file(1)` judging each time.
# `filename: "shot-noext"` (no extension at all) and `"shot.bin"` each wrote PNG; a `.jpg` name
# with `type` left at its png default wrote PNG; and a `.png` name with `type: "jpeg"` wrote
# JPEG. The format comes from the `type` argument (an enum of png|jpeg) and the name is passed
# through untouched. So a screenshot under a name none of these four match walks straight past
# them — and, the other way round, one of them can be "covering" a file that is really the other
# format, which is a second reason the byte-reading half is not optional. The
# name-independent half is in tests/unit/test_repo_browser_isolation.py: it reads the leading
# MAGIC BYTES of what `git add -A` could publish and fails on PNG/JPEG/PDF under ANY name.
#
# AND THOSE THREE ARE NOT THE WHOLE BINARY SURFACE — an earlier draft of this comment said they
# were, and it was disproved by construction. With the DEFAULT capability set `tools/list` shows
# SEVEN tools taking a `filename`, six of which WRITE, and one of them is
# `browser_network_request`: with `part: "response-body"` it drops the RAW BODY of any request the
# page made into the same root, in whatever format the server sent. Measured on a probe page — a
# GIF (`47494638…`) and a ZIP (`504b0304…`) landed as `.bin` files, ignored by nothing and matched
# by no signature here. So the byte scan is complete about the three formats it NAMES, and about
# NAMES; it is not complete about FORMATS, and no version of it could be.
#
# Neither half reaches the text writers either — `browser_snapshot`, `browser_console_messages`,
# `browser_network_requests` and `browser_evaluate` all drop theirs in the root too, carrying the
# page's TEXT and request query strings under names and in formats indistinguishable from a
# legitimate file here. Filed as #703, and NOT closable by a rule: the names measured were `.md`,
# `.txt`, `.json` and one with no extension, while this repo's tracked files are 7 `.md`, 3
# `.json`, 1 `.yml` and 2 with no extension (LICENSE, skills) — any pattern wide enough to catch
# the spill hides the repo's own content, so the fix is a change to what SKILL.md asks agents to
# pass. `*.pdf` is the one rule here that guards a NON-default tool:
# `browser_pdf_save` appears only with `PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_CAPS=pdf` — measured absent from the
# default 24 and present in the 53 with every capability on — so it is defence in depth, exactly
# as the storage-state rules below are for their own capability-gated tool.
#
# PRICE, which is why this is a decision and not a tidy-up: `git add -A` will no longer take a
# legitimate image or PDF. Affordable because this repo has none — measured 2026-08-02,
# `git ls-files` returns nothing for any of the four, and `git log --all --name-only` (broader
# than `--diff-filter=A`: ANY commit touching such a path, on any ref, not just additions)
# returns nothing either. That is the same standing `*.html` above already has, and the present
# tense of it is held by a test rather than by this sentence. A deliberate asset stays
# committable via `git add -f <path>`, which
# refuses out loud first. Case-dependent exactly like the storage-state list below: `shot.PNG` is
# covered on a macOS checkout and NOT on Linux, where CI runs — no leak follows, because the
# magic-byte scan reads bytes rather than names on either platform.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.pdf

# Playwright storage state = LIVE session cookies + localStorage: a credential for whatever
# the browser was logged into, this project's tracker included. THIS REPO IS PUBLIC.
#
# Measured (#585, @playwright/mcp 0.0.78, driven by a purpose-built stdio client against a
# throwaway origin): the only producer is the `browser_storage_state` tool, behind
# PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_CAPS=storage. Given no filename it writes
# `.playwright-mcp/storage-state-<timestamp>.json` — the OUTPUT DIR, not the repo root.
# Given one, it resolves it against the server's cwd, so `state585.json` lands in the root.
# By default it refuses paths outside the MCP client's roots (cwd + cwd/.playwright-mcp) —
# but that is a DEFAULT, not a law: with PLAYWRIGHT_MCP_ALLOW_UNRESTRICTED_FILE_ACCESS=true
# the same call wrote a usable seed straight outside the repo.
#
# WHAT THE LIST BELOW DOES AND DOES NOT DO — read this before trusting it. It excludes a
# fixed set of NAMES: the tool's own default, the spellings a human reaches for, and
# Playwright's documented `playwright/.auth/user.json` convention. That forecloses the
# `git add -A` accident for those names and nothing more. It CANNOT be complete — the tool
# accepts ANY filename anywhere under the root, so e.g. `tracker-login.json` is not covered,
# and no pattern broad enough to catch that is safe to write here. The non-name-based half
# of the guard lives in tests/unit/test_repo_browser_isolation.py: it asks git what
# `git add -A` would publish and fails on any file of storage-state SHAPE under ANY name and at
# ANY SIZE — a candidate too large to read is REPORTED, not skipped. The format carries a
# localStorage array PER ORIGIN, filled from every origin the context visited, so an export has
# no fixed upper size; measured, a correctly-shaped one with a single 4 MiB localStorage entry is
# 4,194,662 bytes. "Too big to classify" is therefore what a fat credential looks like, and the
# first version of this scan capped itself at 1 MiB and let exactly that walk past. It is a GATE
# (red suite before push, red CI after), not a lock on `git commit`.
#
# PRICE of the bare names below, measured with `git check-ignore --no-index -v` rather than read
# off the patterns. `state*.json` is a GLOB, and a pattern with no slash matches at ANY DEPTH, so
# it also hides `states.json`, `state-machine.json`, `state-cache.json`, `statement.json` and
# `src/data/state-defaults.json` — any basename starting with `state`, anywhere in the tree.
# `auth.json`/`cookies.json`/`session.json` are exact basenames but likewise at any depth
# (`pkg/auth.json` too). Only `git add -A` skips them silently; a plain `git add <path>` still
# refuses out loud and points at `-f`.
#
# And this is a DIFFERENT LIST on different machines, because git folds the case of ignore
# patterns only where `core.ignorecase` is true — which it sets from the filesystem at clone
# time: true on macOS (this repo's checkouts, and where the MCP browser that writes these files
# actually runs), false on Linux, where CI runs. Measured with `-c core.ignorecase=false`:
# `storageState.json` — Playwright's OWN spelling, the `storageState` option and
# `context.storageState({path})`, so the likeliest name a reader of the upstream API types —
# is covered here and NOT covered on Linux, as are `Auth.json`, `Cookies.json`, `State.json`.
# No leak follows: the shape scan reads shape, not names, on either platform. Both directions
# are pinned, with `core.ignorecase` forced, so the pins mean the same thing on both.
*storage-state*.json
*storage_state*.json
*storagestate*.json
state*.json
auth.json
cookies.json
session.json
.auth/
